speechtext:
What federal grants have been expended for each province of Canada in each fiscal year since April 1, 1936, under each of the following heads:
(a)	capital grants for educational buildings;
(b)	bursaries or scholarships; (c) apprentice training grants; (d) special grants for equipment or machinery used in vocational, apprentice or non-military or non-veteran youth training; (e) grants for equipment or machinery to retraining of veterans; (f) grants toward instructors' salaries for veteran training in (i) vocational subjects; (ii) pre-university academic instruction; (iii) university instruction?

The minister has
intimated that this is merely enabling legislation and that nothing can be done without the consent of the local authorities. The hon. member for Stanstead has also given me a little legal advice to the effect that this is enabling legislation.
But a reading of the section indicates that it is not merely enabling legislation. It commits us to guaranteeing a return on investment at a rate of interest not to exceed two per cent per annum for a period of years,

National Housing Act
and provides the conditions under which the investment will be made. It states that:
(a)	The land shall, in the opinion of the corporation, be suitable for a residential housing development;
(b)	the purchase price of the said land shall be satisfactory to the corporation;
(c)	the improvements to be effected and the cost thereof shall be satisfactory to the corporation;
(d)	the company shall submit to the corporation an application in a form satisfactory to the corporation containing such information and accompanied by such material as the corporation may prescribe;
(e)	the investment shall first be approved in writing by the corporation.
In view of the unanimous opinion of the house that we must have fuller cooperation on the part of the dominion, provincial and municipal authorities in solving our housing programme, I think we should not pass legislation such as is proposed by the minister without making it clear in black and white just what the municipalities will be committed to, if an act is passed. I think there must be some guarantee that the local authorities will not be saddled with the perpetual cost of maintaining services if some arrangement is entered into between the federal government and t'he life insurance companies to complete a development outside one of our cities. Unless there is some agreement among the people who will be affected, I believe we are heading for trouble. I hope the minister will accept the amendment moved by the hon. member for Regina City.
